            <title><![CDATA[Image of our satellite-choked night skies (117 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://kottke.org/26/11/stunning-image-of-our-satellite-choked-night-skies</link>
            <guid>50379773</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 14:49:07 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://kottke.org/26/11/stunning-image-of-our-satellite-choked-night-skies">https://kottke.org/26/11/stunning-image-of-our-satellite-choked-night-skies</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50379773">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
        

                                                            
            
            	<p><img src="https://kottke.org/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none//images/posts/01/editor-1793852012-b8d841d6.jpg" srcset="https://kottke.org/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=500,metadata=none//images/posts/01/editor-1793852012-b8d841d6.jpg 500w, https://kottke.org/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none//images/posts/01/editor-1793852012-b8d841d6.jpg 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 500px) 500px, 1200px" loading="lazy"></p>
	<p>To create <a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/Dei4FQ0HSt-">this composite image</a>, photographer Brendan Larsen captured almost 600 photographs over a two-hour period and then manually stitched them together.</p>
	<blockquote><p>This is the most time-consuming photo edit I’ve EVER done!</p><p>I left my camera photographing the night sky over Mt Taranaki for nearly TWO HOURS, capturing 578 photographs.</p><p>The result? An estimated 14,450 satellite trail appearances, representing approximately 1,400 individual satellites! 😱</p><p>I manually masked in EVERY SINGLE TRAIL from the original photographs — approximately 45 HOURS of editing to create this one image!</p></blockquote>
	<p>The Guardian has more on how he created the image (which, despite many online commenters’ beliefs, is not AI):</p>
	<blockquote><p>Larsen, 51, says he set his camera up to take an unlimited number of eight-second-long shots, with a two-second gap in between each photo. “I left her running for two hours and captured 578 photos … That was the easy part.”</p><p>The hard part came next. Larsen spent about 45 hours layering the individual photos – each of which contained about 25 satellites – over one another, using Adobe Photoshop. Much of the time was spent painstakingly erasing stars, to reveal the satellites.</p><p>“I could start to see the pattern emerging and realised: ‘Wow, this looks really awesome.’ I knew if I carried on it would look even better and more impressive in terms of satellite numbers.”</p></blockquote>
	<p>Fellow photographer Vincent Laforet <a href="https://vincentlaforet.substack.com/p/still-this-is-an-important-photograph">called Larsen’s image “an important photograph”</a>:</p>
	<blockquote><p>I was initially startled. Then confused. Then fascinated all in quick succession.</p><p>And then I felt a deep sense of betrayal once I realized that what I was tracking with my eyes were a series of small satellites. And btw: that they likely had ruined my long exposure that I was capturing at the time.</p><p>I remember asking in the moment:</p><p>Who gave anyone permission do this?</p></blockquote>


                            <ul>
                                            <li><a href="https://kottke.org/tag/astronomy">astronomy</a></li>
                                            <li><a href="https://kottke.org/tag/Brendan%20Larsen">Brendan Larsen</a></li>
                                            <li><a href="https://kottke.org/tag/photography">photography</a></li>
                                            <li><a href="https://kottke.org/tag/satellites">satellites</a></li>
                                            <li><a href="https://kottke.org/tag/Vincent%20Laforet">Vincent Laforet</a></li>
                                    </ul>
            
                    
        
    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
