            <title><![CDATA[I wish tech companies knew more about me (157 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://rakhim.exotext.com/i-wish-tech-companies-knew-more-about-me</link>
            <guid>50378942</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 13:40:52 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://rakhim.exotext.com/i-wish-tech-companies-knew-more-about-me">https://rakhim.exotext.com/i-wish-tech-companies-knew-more-about-me</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378942">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
            <p>"Tech companies are spying on us" is a truism at this point. Endless articles and investigations have ensured that everybody is aware: they are spying on us, they listen to our conversations, they know more about us than we know ourselves, they know you're going to be fired or become pregnant or become depressed before it happens...</p>
<p>I have never felt anything close to that.</p>
<p>I get shown ads for products I just purchased. All this spying, machine learning, big data result in "he bought a mattress, show him more mattresses". Here, I literally just opened a random website with no adblocker, and I see a purple gamepad I recently purchased, with two other gamepads:</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/114f4789-cf10-4af1-bdb0-99992aaa7130.png" alt=""></p>
<p>When listening to personalized playlists on Spotify and YouTube Music I skip every single electronic track with vocals. I listen to a lot of techno and related genres, and the moment someone starts singing I skip the track. I've been doing it for years. None of these advanced algorithms have ever noticed this simple pattern.</p>
<p>Using Google Chrome, logged into my Google account, I searched for info about Google AI plan usage limits, got an AI-generated response with a link to the help page, clicked it and ended up, logged out, on a Finnish-language landing page (because I'm located in Finland).</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/f052c14e-a94a-4877-b2b2-02d985251128.png" alt=""></p>
<p>My user account, system locale, browser locale, and keyboard settings are all set to English. I am logged into Chrome and all Google services. </p>
<p>I travel to Germany every few months, and lots of services that I've been using for years suddenly decide I speak German. </p>
<p>The PlayStation online store is in Finnish, while some menus are in English, and even if I switch the website language to English (via a hidden, clunky selector), it resets back after a few clicks or after opening a new tab. </p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/070ed5af-59fd-4d5d-8182-b013f953396b.png" alt=""></p>
<p>Here's an ad for some smart home wellness system. I click on it: they don't deliver to Finland. </p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/71e6059e-f9d0-41d7-ac62-1d7ac8b9f3dc.png" alt=""></p>
<p>A full page of repeating ads for industrial-grade light-curing, methacrylate-based resin designed for 3D printing. I don't have a 3D printer and have no idea what this is.</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/a7b8a2e2-280a-464c-afd4-7494ff6b358c.jpg" alt=""></p>
<p>I clicked on that ad; now I keep getting other 3D-printing-related ads. </p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/5b76699e-2018-4c8d-9e12-629c2c0349de.png" alt=""></p>
<p>Here's an ad for an email/WhatsApp marketing tool. I never needed any marketing tool, nor do I own a WhatsApp business account.</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/2d588940-257d-4df7-9eee-507fdb808e59.png" alt=""></p>
<p>Now it's a Helsinki hotel. I live in Helsinki, I haven't searched for local hotels since 2014 when I first arrived here.</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/11cb5e16-7220-4f46-8f43-df0a287eedce.png" alt=""></p>
<p>A fucking asphalt cleaning tractor.</p>
<p><img src="https://img.exotext.com/1/8d14364e-e6ce-48ca-bf6f-d8f7373179df.jpg" alt=""></p>
<p>I searched YouTube for installation instruction for a specific appliance "AEG CCS84751", watched a couple of relevant videos and the next day saw my homepage populated with more videos about random ovens and stoves. OK, so, let's see, a user searches for installation instructions for a very specific, concrete model. What could we machine-learn from this, what are the options here?</p>
<ol>
<li>The user is into ovens</li>
<li>The user is into installing appliances</li>
<li>The user needs this one particular instruction for this one particular oven because the user maybe bought that oven and wants to install it, once, maybe?</li>
</ol>
<p>My bank keeps sending me offers for car insurance. I have car insurance with that bank. Have had it for years.</p>
<p>An airline, when reminding me of an upcoming trip, send info about visas and border crossing laws which don't apply to my citizenship. They literally have a scan of my passport.</p>
<p>Netflix on Apple TV asks me to select a user every time I launch it. I've  had this account for 15+ years, and never had more than one user on it. I always select the only option there is to select. There is no setting to disable this screen.</p>
<p>My mobile provider keeps sending me discounts for children's products and events from their corporate partners. I don't have children.</p>
<p>HBO keeps recommending me foreign movies and shows which I cannot watch as they don't have English subtitles.</p>
<p>I have no doubts that a lot of information is being tracked, stored, and sold. Since that's already done, could you guys at least use some of it?..</p>

            </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Discord is ditching Electron and its new app uses less RAM (267 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.theverge.com/news/1027379/discord-ditching-electron-web-app</link>
            <guid>50378625</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 13:05:28 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/1027379/discord-ditching-electron-web-app">https://www.theverge.com/news/1027379/discord-ditching-electron-web-app</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378625">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><div><p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/authors/tom-warren"><img alt="Tom Warren" data-chromatic="ignore" loading="lazy" width="36" height="36" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" srcset="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/Tom_BLURPLE.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=48 1x, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/Tom_BLURPLE.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=96 2x" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/Tom_BLURPLE.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=96"></a></p><div><p><span aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true" role="button" tabindex="0"><span id="follow-author-standard_article_details-dmcyOmF1dGhvclByb2ZpbGU6MTY0"><span><span><svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 9 9" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Follow"><path d="M5 0H4V4H0V5H4V9H5V5H9V4H5V0Z"></path></svg></span></span><span>Tom Warren</span></span></span></p> <p><span>is a senior correspondent and author of <a href="https://www.theverge.com/notepad-microsoft-newsletter"><i>Notepad</i></a>, who has been covering all things Microsoft, PC, and tech for over 20 years.</span></p></div></div><div id="zephr-anchor"><p>Discord is finally giving up on Electron, the cross-platform framework that lets developers build desktop apps using web technologies. Electron web apps are notoriously heavy on system resources, and Discord is now moving its desktop app fully to Rust — a modern programming language designed for speed and memory safety.</p><p>“We’ve been early adopters of Rust since 2018 and a lot of our infra, voice tech, and even some of the current client is already Rust, the holy grail was getting the whole client there,” explains Discord chief technology officer Stanislav Vishnevskiy in a <a href="https://x.com/svishnevskiy/status/2118081920194203799">post on X</a>. “That means Electron goes away, and so does React Native, mobile keeps native UI but shares app code with desktop via Rust.”</p><p>Vishnevskiy also shared an image of the Discord Windows client using just 290MB of RAM, which is a clear improvement as you only have to participate in a few servers and RAM usage can easily hit 1GB or more using the current Windows app.</p><p>It’s not clear when Discord will make the full switch over to Rust, but Vishnevskiy says the company “hope to get it into testing with users soon.”</p><div><p><span><strong>Follow topics and authors</strong> from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates.</span></p><ul><li id="follow-author-article_footer-dmcyOmF1dGhvclByb2ZpbGU6MTY0"><span aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true" role="button" tabindex="0"><span><span><svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 9 9" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Follow"><path d="M5 0H4V4H0V5H4V9H5V5H9V4H5V0Z"></path></svg></span><span>Tom Warren</span></span></span></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li></ul></div></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Download your favourite YouTube videos (185 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://criminallyvulgar.dev/you-should-absolutely-download-your-favourite-youtube-videos/</link>
            <guid>50378571</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 13:00:11 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://criminallyvulgar.dev/you-should-absolutely-download-your-favourite-youtube-videos/">https://criminallyvulgar.dev/you-should-absolutely-download-your-favourite-youtube-videos/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378571">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
    

    

    
        

        <p>
            <i>
                <time datetime="2026-10-13T17:51Z">
    13 Oct, 2026
</time>
            </i>
        </p>
    

    <p>Signal boosting <a href="https://blog.lauramichet.com/you-should-just-download-your-favorite-youtube-content-and-put-it-on-a-hard-drive-in-your-home/">Laura's post</a> that you should absolutely download and archive the stuff that matters to you. The corporate internet is hostile to art and the things you love do not belong to you unless you claim them for yourself. If you've been around for a while on YouTube, just look at your "liked videos" playlist and see how many of the early ones are no longer available (something you literally have to enable in the playlist settings, it's that endemic).</p>
<p>The tools to do this in a basic way are extremely easy to get hold of, and generally have good defaults. I'm also going to talk a little about my own setup, and why it is the weird way it is, but it's definitely overkill for most setups. <a href="https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp">yt-dlp</a> or one of it's <a href="https://yt-dlp.app/">many</a> <a href="https://stacher.io/">wrappers</a> <sup id="fnref-1"><a href="#fn-1">1</a></sup> will do most of what you need, and a folder on a bulk storage drive or NAS will be plenty sufficient to make sure you always have access to them.</p>
<p>For me though, I had a particular need - I have unfortunately programmed myself to be almost entirely unable to fall asleep without a <a href="https://criminallyvulgar.dev/many-a-true-nerd-appreciation-post/">Many a True Nerd</a> Let's Play on a low volume.</p>
<p>This comes with a couple of issues.</p>
<ol>
<li>I need to be able to play it on my TV.</li>
<li>It has to integrate into my existing NAS infrastructure.</li>
<li>I need to be able to organise hundreds of videos across dozens of playlists. This is a bigger sticking point than it sounds.</li>
<li>Adding new ones or placing them can't be a manual job. Again, hundreds of videos.</li>
</ol>
<p>My NAS media library is built around Jellyfin, which <em>has</em> a concept of Playlists but they're really not intended as a first-order organisational structure. You're expected to take a few tracks from a bunch of existing albums and place them in a playlist separate from the actual <strong>Library</strong>. No, Jellyfin has a very rigid concept of either Movies, TV Shows &gt; Seasons &gt; Episodes, or Artists &gt; Albums &gt; Songs.</p>
<p>TV Shows is the obvious analogue here, and with some really wild finagling I was able to get a setup together that is good enough to satisfy for now<sup id="fnref-2"><a href="#fn-2">2</a></sup>. My setup looks like this:</p>
<h2 id="a-hrefhttpsjellyfinorgjellyfina"><a href="https://jellyfin.org/">Jellyfin</a></h2><p>In Jellyfin I have a Library setup in TV Shows mode called YouTube. I've turned off all metadata scrapers and the like, and have it set to prefer file data over file names.</p>
<h2 id="a-hrefhttpsgithubcomjmbannonytdl-subytdl-suba"><a href="https://github.com/jmbannon/ytdl-sub">ytdl-sub</a></h2><p>This is the only yt-dlp wrapper that allowed me to properly maintain the Playlist structure to any extent, and is entirely based on a series of .yaml files. In it's self-hosted docker-compose form, it comes with a web-app VSCode IDE which is honestly super handy - bridges the tech-comfort gap nicely.</p>
<p>I have a custom genre and list of MATN playlists I want it to collect and set metadata for. <img src="https://bear-images.sfo2.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com/criminallyvulgar/ytdlp-sub-subs.webp" alt="My list of playlists and videos"></p>
<p>You can also see there I have a handful of Folding Ideas videos set as "Documentaries" - this is more in keeping with how ytdl-sub expects to be used.</p>
<p>The truth is that 99% of youtubers, and 99.9% of viewers, don't particularly need to structure everything as playlists within a channel. Most have a much higher proportion of one-off to series videos that make my MATN structure utterly pointless. As such, ytdl-sub defaults to tagging videos with a Season field organised by Year, but it stores them in folders named for the Playlist. This leads to Jellyfin putting every one of those playlist videos in 2 places within the channel - a Year Series, and a Playlist Series:<img src="https://bear-images.sfo2.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com/criminallyvulgar/jellyfin_matn.webp" alt="My MATN Jellyfin Page"></p>
<p>I only recently realised how to fix this, which is to assign the Playlist title as the Season Number in my ytdl-sub config file - it seems like it should break, but it doesn't: <img src="https://bear-images.sfo2.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com/criminallyvulgar/ytdlp-sub-channel.webp" alt="my config file in ytdl-sub"></p>
<p>I could write a script to go in and fix this for all my existing series, but the issue is that once Jellyfin has Imported a video, I can't get it to update the Season Number without fully reimporting the Library, something I'm not really interested in doing for a terabyte of video. Oh, speaking of storage size...</p>
<h2 id="a-hrefhttpstdarriotdarra"><a href="https://tdarr.io/">Tdarr</a></h2><p>Tdarr is a service that can automatically run through a library of videos and re-encode it in whatever way you desire - usually to some smaller, more efficient format or codec. I use it to encode everything in my media server to HEVC/H.265 as storage is my main bottleneck - processing power is the tradeoff and I have that in spades.</p>
<p>I'm honestly not going to recommend Tdarr unless you are <em>very</em> comfortable looking at a massively complex and poorly designed UI and making the smart decision to do the minimum. Everyone I know who has used it has come in with grand ambitions of brilliant complex workflows and settled on just using the default one because it works fine.</p>
<p>It has a tendency to break on anything remotely complex, and I had to manually tweak a script so that it wouldn't cry any time there was a data stream, but once it gets going it just chugs along re-encoding gigabyte after gigabyte.</p>
<p>It does seem to cause Jellyfin to lose Thumbnail images for reasons I haven't fully figured out, but it's generally fixable either manually, with a "refresh metadata", or in your media management software of choice.</p>
<p>It saved me about 700GB across my 2TB library, though I probably could have avoided some of that cost by just being more picky about my downloads.</p>
<p>Again, I don't really recommend it! But if you're in a pinch, it will absolutely do the job.</p>
<hr>
<p><a href="mailto:crim@criminallyvulgar.dev?subject=Re:%20You%20should%20absolutely%20download%20your%20favourite%20youtube%20videos">✉️ Reply via email</a></p>
<section>
<ol>
<li id="fn-1"><p>These are probably rife with vibe-coding, as yt-dlp scripts are well documented online and using the command line is daunting for most.<a href="#fnref-1">↩</a></p></li>
<li id="fn-2"><p>As with all things homelab - it's never perfect, which keeps the hobby alive forever.<a href="#fnref-2">↩</a></p></li>
</ol>
</section>


    

    
        
            <p>
                
                    <a rel="nofollow" href="https://criminallyvulgar.dev/blog/?q=blog">#blog</a>
                
                    <a rel="nofollow" href="https://criminallyvulgar.dev/blog/?q=homelab">#homelab</a>
                
            </p>
        

        
            



        

        
            
        
    


  </div></div>]]></description>
