        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Obscure audio codec is a perfect fit for the N64 (134 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://phoboslab.org/log/2026/11/n64-ulc-codec</link>
            <guid>50378508</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 12:51:48 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://phoboslab.org/log/2026/11/n64-ulc-codec">https://phoboslab.org/log/2026/11/n64-ulc-codec</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378508">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>	
	<div>
		
		<p>— Tuesday, November 3rd 2026</p>
	</div>
	

	<p>Sorry for the clickbait title. I promise this post delivers: 44100 Hz stereo 
with near-Opus bitrates using about 4% of the N64’s CPU time.</p>
<p>But first, some history: The options for playing and storing audio data on the N64 were dire.
The PlayStation could just stream uncompressed redbook audio straight from the 
CD. On the N64, cartridge space was at a premium and time on the 93 MHz CPU was
limited.</p>
<p>Worse, access to the vector co-processor, the RSP, was guarded by Nintendo for
the longest time. Nintendo's own platform library, Libultra, internally used
the RSP for various graphics and sound-related tasks, but Nintendo didn't allow
third-party developers to directly write code for it.</p>
<p>Libultra provided some functions to transparently play back compressed audio
using an ADPCM scheme (adaptive differential pulse code modulation). The RSP
handled decompression on the fly, just before the audio was sent to the output
buffer.</p>
<p>This works well enough and cuts the space needed for a sample by a factor of 4.
Still, a 5-minute stereo audio track at 44100 Hz will land at
about 12MB – way too much for an N64 cartridge, even when you cut the sample rate
in half. The whole Mario 64 ROM is just 8MB in total. The biggest N64 games 
were 64MB.</p>
<p>So N64 games produced music similarly to a tracker: short samples were 
scheduled and played back with some limited effects at the right time. With
the space constraints on the cartridge, fully “recorded” audio was pretty much
out of the question.</p>
<p>Later in the N64's life, some blessed developers were allowed by Nintendo
to directly access the RSP. Most famously, Conker's Bad Fur Day used this to
decompress MP3 audio for the myriad of speech samples in the game. Decoding MP3
is quite expensive and the frame rate of the game suffered. It was good enough
for cutscenes, but just too much for continuous background music or sound 
effects during gameplay.</p>
<h2>Audio in the Open Source Libdragon</h2>
<p>Similar to Nintendo's
Libultra, <a href="https://github.com/n64brew/libdragon">Libdragon</a>, the open source
alternative, has a real-time ADPCM decoder running on the RSP. So with ROM space
being cheaper these days, I just compressed all music for <a href="https://phoboslab.org/log/2026/08/xibalba64-making-of">Xibalba
64</a> with ADPCM. About 31MB of the 32MB ROM
are used for sound and music. Remember that Mario 64 just used an 8MB ROM and
Xibalba 64 is a much simpler game with only 15 minutes of music. It worked,
but storing music this way is a gratuitous waste of space.</p>
<p>Now, Libdragon also has a real-time, RSP-accelerated Opus decoder. It's a marvel
in itself. The Opus format was published 16 years after the N64 and yet
Giovanni Bajo managed to make it work on this <em>“obsolete”</em> hardware. Compression
and audio quality are excellent, but again – it's too slow to be used during
gameplay.</p>
<p>What's the alternative then? We could build an RSP-accelerated MP3 decoder, 
but the performance gain over Opus would be marginal, and quality and compression
would be worse.</p>
<p><a href="https://phoboslab.org/log/2023/02/qoa-time-domain-audio-compression">QOA</a> would be a slight 
improvement over ADPCM, but it's just a poor fit for the vector co-processor: 
each sample relies on the previous decoded sample. Hard to vectorize.</p>
<p>A simple transform codec, something <em>like</em> MP3, but without all the 
design-by-consortium baggage, may work…</p>
<h2>The Ultra Low Complexity Codec</h2>
<p>During some late-night <em>“research”</em>, I stumbled across an old Game Boy
Advance demo that plays recorded music at just 70kbps. That's less than a
typical MP3, but at comparable quality. I repeat: On a GBA. With a 16 MHz ARM CPU. With no
vector instructions and, according to the GitHub page, some
headroom to spare.</p>

<p><em><a href="https://github.com/Aikku93/ulcplayer-gba">ulcplayer-gba</a> by Aikku93, recorded in a Game Boy Advance emulator</em></p><p>Time-domain codecs (such as ADPCM) need significantly higher bitrates for this
quality. Subband codecs, such as MP2, have much worse quality at comparable
bitrates, and more “modern” transform codecs, such as MP3, usually
<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b0zZpzxHSeM">need at least an 80–100 MHz CPU</a>. For
this GBA demo, no existing codec would fit the bill.</p>
<p>So <a href="https://github.com/Aikku93">Aikku93</a> not only wrote a decoder in ARM assembly, 
but also created a whole new codec: The 
<a href="https://github.com/Aikku93/ulc-codec">Ultra Low Complexity Codec</a> (ULC).</p>
<p>ULC <em>is</em> a transform codec and somewhat similar to MP3. ULC transforms slices of 
PCM data into the frequency domain. Like MP3, it uses the Modified Discrete Cosine
Transform (MDCT) to do so. But unlike MP3, it does not split the signal
into multiple bands (ULC's MDCT is “full-band”). Where MP3 uses classic
entropy encoding (Huffman specifically), ULC does… not. Instead, transformed
coefficients are stored with a very simple nibble-based syntax.</p>
<p>In short: ULC is what MP3 could have been. The same core idea, but without the 
cruft around it. The “ultra low complexity” does not come from a different 
algorithm, but by carefully applying and packaging it.</p>
<p>The nibble-based packaging is maybe the most elegant idea of ULC. Each 
coefficient is stored in just 4 bits, interpreted as a signed integer. Some 
values are reserved to indicate zero fill, noise fill, or a quantizer change:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>-7 … -2</code>, <code>2 … 7</code> - coefficient value</li>
<li><code>0</code> - zero run, run length (<code>1 … 16</code>) follows in the next nibble</li>
<li><code>1</code> - long zero run, run length (<code>17 … 288</code>) follows in the next two nibbles</li>
<li><code>-1</code> - quantizer change, value follows in the next nibble</li>
<li><code>-8</code> - noise fill, length and amplitude follow in the next three nibbles</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything is nibble aligned. This means the whole codec works without any
arbitrary bit reads or entropy decoding. Reading and
unpacking a block of coefficients takes just <a href="https://github.com/Aikku93/ulc-codec/blob/b47df8d50c2ef2d765e5dbec0bf567b5e558173a/libulc/ulcDecoder.c#L99-L197">~150 lines of C</a>.</p>
<p>Also note that noise fill is a valuable addition. Cymbals, hi-hats and snare
drums greatly benefit from that. MP3 does not have anything similar and 
needs to encode quasi-random coefficients <em>as is</em>. </p>
<p>ULC can produce <em>usable</em> results even at 16kbps. Quality of course suffers, but
it might still be good enough for some applications.</p>
<p>Aikku93 has been working on ULC since 2019 and has steadily improved the encoding
quality. All code is permissively licensed. Time to try this on the N64 then!</p>
<h2>ULC on the N64</h2>
<p>I spent an evening in pre-LLM days compiling the ULC reference decoder for the
N64 and producing a small demo. It worked without a hitch.</p>
<p>The only problem: it was keeping the CPU very busy. Nothing was offloaded to the 
RSP. As a proof of concept, it wasn't doing much: we already knew the 93 MHz CPU
was enough to decode a transform-based codec, but we don't need a “music player”
that does nothing else. We need this for background music and spoken dialog
during gameplay. It <em>cannot</em> hog the CPU.</p>
<h3>Float to 16-bit Fixed Point</h3>
<p>Obviously the IMDCT needs to be moved to the RSP coprocessor instead. We
could just DMA one block of decoded coefficients to the RSP's memory, do the
transform and DMA the completed samples back to RDRAM into the output buffer.</p>
<p>That's easier said than done, though. The RSP has only 4096 bytes of data memory (DMEM) 
and another 4096 bytes for instructions (IMEM). The vector instructions work in
16-bit or 32-bit fixed point, with no floats. The IMDCT will only be efficient
if all the data for a transform fits into the RSP's DMEM at once. We also need some of the
DMEM for temporary values and – if possible – the lapping buffer: half of the 
previously decoded block. So with the default 2048-sample block size of ULC,
we're out of luck, even with 16-bit fixed point.</p>
<p>While ULC can work with smaller MDCT block sizes, it was optimized for 2048, so
we should keep it as large as possible. On top of that, the ULC reference 
encoder/decoder uses floats, so the obvious choice would be 32-bit fixed point to
maintain quality, but 16-bit <em>may</em> work. So we have two options, both arriving
at <code>3072 bytes</code>:</p>
<ol>
<li>32-bit values, a 512-sample block size (256-sample lap buffer): <code>(512 + 256) * 4 bytes</code></li>
<li>16-bit values, a 1024-sample block size (512-sample lap buffer): <code>(1024 + 512) * 2 bytes</code></li>
</ol>
<p>Using 16-bit fixed point would not only reduce the DMA traffic, but also speed up
computation on the RSP. The trade-off would be a higher noise floor. How high?
No idea. So I carefully refactored the reference decoder, still running on the
N64 CPU, to work in 16-bit fixed point (Q1.14).</p>
<p>After almost blowing my ears off a bunch of times, I got it working and could 
only hear (or imagine?) the faintest white noise when cranking it up. Case 
settled: 16-bit it is.</p>
<h3>Cramming it into the RSP</h3>
<p>Now I <em>just</em> needed to port this C code to the RSP. My knowledge of MIPS
assembly is very basic, and writing an RSP decoder would be a serious endeavor.</p>
<p>This is where I took a hiatus from this project – I had a 
<a href="https://phoboslab.org/log/2026/08/xibalba64-making-of">game</a> to finish. When I returned earlier this 
year LLMs have improved quite a bit. Still, I didn't
exactly trust GPT-5 with it. In some previous experiments it did a very
bad job, often ignoring delay slots and other intricacies of the RSP, never
finishing the job.</p>
<p><em>(Aside: GPT-6 or Opus-5 would probably one-shot this now)</em></p>
<p>So instead I opted for a more iterative approach. Max Bebök created a small
C-like language to write code for the RSP, aptly named 
<a href="https://github.com/HailToDodongo/rspl">RSPL</a>. Armed with the language 
documentation, I asked Codex to translate the C code to RSPL piece by piece,
always verifying the result. It took a few days to port and land on an API 
design that made sense. Then in subsequent steps Codex further optimized the 
RSPL assembly output by first rewriting some parts (directly in assembly this time),
then minimizing pipeline stalls by <a href="https://github.com/phoboslab/rspopt">adapting</a>
the RSPL's optimizer.</p>
<p>The end result is <a href="https://github.com/n64brew/libdragon/blob/preview/src/audio/rsp_ulc.S">~1400 lines of MIPS assembly</a>
handling the IMDCT, lapping, stereo interleaving and output scaling. It barely
fits into the 4096 byte IMEM of the RSP.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://github.com/n64brew/libdragon/blob/preview/src/audio/wav64_ulc.c">C side of the decoder</a> 
is still handwritten, but only because LLMs have no taste. The whole process
took me a few weeks, but I'm still amazed that it worked at all.</p>

<p><em>My <a href="https://github.com/phoboslab/ulc64/">ulc64 demo</a> demonstrating quality at various bitrates</em></p><p>To make ULC easily seekable, I added a sparse table at the end of the
file that contains the byte offset of every eighth frame. The decoder quickly skips
ahead from the nearest frame to the desired one. This adds about 20 bytes per
second – manageable even for 16kbps audio.</p>
<p>ULC proved to be a <em>very</em> viable alternative to ADPCM and Opus on the N64. 
Quality and bitrate are close to those of Opus, but complexity is only 4x that of ADPCM. A
single 44100 Hz stereo track costs about 4% of CPU time and 5% of RSP time.
Perfectly usable for background music, speech, ambience and longer sound effects.</p>
<p>The decoder works transparently in Libdragon, just like all other audio codecs. 
It has been merged into the <a href="https://github.com/n64brew/libdragon/tree/preview">preview branch</a> a while ago.</p>
<h2>Addendum</h2>
<p>A few days ago an excellent port of Half-Life for the N64 was published. 
The <code>README</code> states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[...] libdragon's ULC audio codec for the 2,500 sounds and 27 music tracks, 
is what makes everything fit in one 64 MB cartridge image.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>~ <a href="https://github.com/ToxyCola/Half-Life-Nintendo-64-Port">github.com/ToxyCola/Half-Life-Nintendo-64-Port</a></p>
<iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/QhxXwX_wnVI" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen=""></iframe>
<p>Many thanks to <a href="https://github.com/Aikku93">Aikku93</a> for creating ULC. It's a 
wonderful codec. I merely found and adapted it. I seek simplicity and elegance
in everything I do and ULC is something I can truly adore. Kudos!</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Sshole (1743 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://sshhole.com</link>
            <guid>50378228</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 12:19:02 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://sshhole.com">https://sshhole.com</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50378228">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page">

<header>
  <div>
    
    <p>so openssh on musl linux (alpine, openwrt, buildroot, yocto, and a lot of the docker images you've pulled) has had a little problem since 2016.</p>
    <p>accounts that should never take a password at all (root, your key-only deploy user, ldap users, the default user on alpine's aws and azure images) take any password longer than 256 characters.</p>
    <p aria-label="yes, any password. yes. i know. yes."><span>yes, any password.</span> <span>yes.</span> <span>i know.</span> <span>yes.</span></p>
  </div>
  <figure id="hero-term" aria-label="terminal recording of the attack">
    
    <pre><code id="term-code"></code><span></span></pre>
  </figure>
  
</header>



<div id="scrolly">

    <article data-step="0">
      <p>1980</p>
      <h2>the first star</h2>
      <p>back in old unix your password hash just sat in <code>/etc/passwd</code>, right next to your username. in a file everyone could read.</p>
      <p>in 1980 bill joy put a <code>*</code> there for an account called <code>daemon</code>. nobody's supposed to ever log in as it.</p>
      <p>a <code>*</code> isn't a hash, so no password can match it. unix still does this today</p>
      <a href="https://github.com/dspinellis/unix-history-repo/blob/BSD-3/etc/passwd" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>3bsd · /etc/passwd · 1980</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>github.com/dspinellis/unix-history-repo/blob/BSD-3/etc/passwd</span></p><img width="2560" height="480" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/3bsd-passwd.png" alt="daemon:*:1:1:The devil himself:/:" loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>the next field is the real name. he wrote "the devil himself"</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="1">
      <p>1990s</p>
      <h2>how sshd checks your password</h2>
      <p>ok so these days the hash lives in <code>/etc/shadow</code> and looks like <code>$6$abc$k2P9…</code>. the <code>$6$</code> means sha512, <code>abc</code> is the salt, the rest is the actual hash</p>
      <p>you type your password, sshd hashes it with that same salt and checks if it comes out identical. same = you're in. that's it</p>
      <p>and if the field is just <code>*</code>? there's nothing to match. type whatever you want, it's never getting you in</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="2">
      <p>1990s</p>
      <h2>the fine print</h2>
      <p>one more rule. if <code>crypt()</code> can't do its job, it returns NULL. like if the salt asks for some algorithm the system doesn't have, there's nothing to compute, so you get NULL instead of a hash</p>
      <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/7908799/xsh/crypt.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>the single unix spec · crypt() · 1997</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/7908799/xsh/crypt.html</span></p><img width="3936" height="1000" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/posix-crypt.png" alt="RETURN VALUE: Upon successful completion, crypt() returns a pointer to the encoded string. Otherwise it returns a null pointer and sets errno to indicate the error." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>pretty clear.</p>
      <p>except a lot of programs never check for NULL. they just compare whatever <code>crypt()</code> gives back with the stored field, hit the NULL, and crash</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="3">
      <p>2012</p>
      <h2>crypt stops saying NULL</h2>
      <p>so musl (the libc under alpine) goes ok fine, <code>crypt()</code> just won't return NULL anymore. if it fails, it returns a single character instead. that character can never match a real password, so programs don't crash, they just see a wrong password</p>
      <p>now guess which character they picked for that</p>
      <p><span>"</span><span>"</span></p>
      <p>yep. the star. again.</p>
      <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=b3c4cc121f70faea45389fe7ddc1127ed5cbd8bb" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl · b3c4cc12 · aug 2012</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=b3c4cc12</span></p><img width="3280" height="1320" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-b3c4cc12.png" alt="make crypt return an unmatchable hash rather than NULL on failure. always &quot;succeeding&quot; but giving an unmatchable hash is reportedly a better behavior than failing on error." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>"reportedly."</p>
      <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=b3c4cc121f70faea45389fe7ddc1127ed5cbd8bb" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl · b3c4cc12 · the diff</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=b3c4cc12</span></p><img width="3040" height="1072" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-b3c4cc12-diff.png" alt="diff of crypt_des.c: removed the comment 'Should not happen.' and 'return NULL;', added: return (setting[0]=='*') ? &quot;x&quot; : &quot;*&quot;;" loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>"should not happen" gone, NULL gone, <code>"*"</code> in</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="4">
      <p>2012</p>
      <h2>three weeks later</h2>
      <p>then musl adds sha512 crypt. the thing is, sha512crypt gets slower the longer the password is, so someone could send one giant password and keep your server busy for a long time. so musl just caps it at 256 bytes and refuses anything longer</p>
      <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=88bf5a8a8d7d796f63cca8589f4de67aa8345f1a" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl · 88bf5a8a · aug 29 2012</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=88bf5a8a</span></p><img width="3280" height="1440" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-88bf5a8a.png" alt="add sha256/sha512 crypt. Rich Felker, 2012-08-29." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>twenty days after the "*" commit btw</p>
      <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/tree/src/misc/crypt_sha512.c?id=88bf5a8a8d7d796f63cca8589f4de67aa8345f1a#n189" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl · crypt_sha512.c @ 88bf5a8a</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/tree/src/misc/crypt_sha512.c?id=88bf5a8a</span></p><img width="3040" height="2080" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-keymax.png" alt="key limit is not part of the original design, added for DoS protection. #define KEY_MAX 256. if (i > KEY_MAX) return 0;" loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>so past 256 bytes crypt fails. and when crypt fails… it says <code>"*"</code>.</p>
      <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/tree/src/crypt/crypt_sha512.c#n8" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl · crypt_sha512.c</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/tree/src/crypt/crypt_sha512.c</span></p><img width="3040" height="800" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-sha512-header.png" alt="on error &quot;*&quot; is returned." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>literally in the comment the whole time</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="5">
      <p>2016</p>
      <h2>sshd borrows bob's salt</h2>
      <p>CVE-2016-6210. ask sshd about a user that doesn't exist and it says no instantly, cause there's nothing to hash. real users take a few ms longer. so just time it and you know who exists</p>
      <p>the fix is kinda funny. no real hash to check? sshd grabs some other account's salt and hashes your password anyway, just to waste the same amount of time. still a no, just a slower no</p>
            <a href="http://lists.mindrot.org/pipermail/openssh-commits/2016-July/005428.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>openssh-commits · jul 2016</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>lists.mindrot.org/pipermail/openssh-commits/2016-July/005428.html</span></p><img width="3280" height="1440" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/openssh-9286875a7.png" alt="Determine appropriate salt for invalid users." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>"for invalid users." and also, it turns out, real users with a <code>*</code>.</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="6">
      <p>2019 → 2022</p>
      <h2>the fix for the fix</h2>
      <p><b>2019.</b> turns out alpine's docker images had no root password. at all. CVE-2019-5021. alpine fixes it by locking root with <code>!</code></p>
      <p>but without pam, <code>!</code> also kills ssh keys. and cloud images log in with keys.</p>
      <p><b>2022.</b> alpine 3.16 switches root to <code>*</code>. still locked, keys work again</p>
      <a href="https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports/-/commit/fbe926afc76de76e07d078f266badc4144dde8e3" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>alpine aports · fbe926af · shipped in 3.16, 2022</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports/-/commit/fbe926af.patch</span></p><img width="2800" height="1800" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/alpine-fbe926af.png" alt="this still forbids password login but allows logins via ssh keys with pam disabled. # make sure root login is disabled. root:! becomes root:*" loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>narrator: it did not.</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="7">
      <p>2016 → 2026</p>
      <h2>ten quiet years</h2>
      <p>why did nobody notice? well, maybe someone did. maybe someone pasted the wrong thing into a password prompt, half a slack message or a whole ssh key, and it just let them in. you've never pasted the wrong thing into a password prompt?? amazing. a fresh box with no passwords set isn't affected. a <code>*</code> in shadow needs a wrong try first. openssh tests on openbsd and glibc, musl doesn't test sshd, and most distros go through pam, which never even calls crypt for a <code>*</code>.</p>
      <p>so as far as anyone knows, nobody noticed. as far as anyone knows.</p>
      <p>some people got really close though</p>
      <a href="https://bugs.debian.org/714219" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>libc-alpha · debian #714219 · 2013</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>sourceware.org/legacy-ml/libc-alpha/2013-07/msg00046.html</span></p><img width="3040" height="1200" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/libc-alpha-2013.png" alt="The scheme of returning * or x, which are never valid matches, comes from dalias from musl, and I think it is the best compromise/solution." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>a debian dev asks glibc to copy musl's <code>"*"</code>. six years later debian moves to libxcrypt, which says <code>"*0"</code>, and closes the bug. one character away.</p>
      <a href="https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2024/12/31/2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>musl mailing list · dec 2024</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>openwall.com/lists/musl/2024/12/31/2</span></p><img width="3040" height="1280" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/musl-list-2024.png" alt="Sure there's no reason it couldn't have been 257 or 300 or whatever, but either way you'd get things like this when software has bugs in handling the condition." loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>this thread is about busybox <code>passwd</code> breaking on a 257 character password. same <code>"*"</code>, same limit. nobody says "sshd"</p>
      <a href="http://lists.mindrot.org/pipermail/openssh-commits/2026-October/013271.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><p><span>openssh-commits · 30 oct 2026</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></p><p><b>Address</b><span>lists.mindrot.org/pipermail/openssh-commits/2026-October/013271.html</span></p><img width="3280" height="1440" src="https://sshhole.com/receipts/openssh-84f8f4666.png" alt="fix fake password salt generation for bcrypt hashes" loading="lazy"></a>
      <p>a security review fixes <code>pick_salt()</code>. the bug is four lines below it. four days before i found it.</p>
    </article>

    <article data-step="8">
      <p>nov 2026</p>
      <h2>how i ran into it</h2>
      <p>i was messing around with openssh, mostly the boring parts where it talks to libc, like the password code. people don't really look at that stuff, they look at the crypto. so i built sshd every way i could think of (glibc, musl, pam, no pam, shadow, no shadow) and threw like 17,600 weird logins at it. weird hashes, broken accounts, passwords way too long</p>
      <p>oh did i say "i"? sorry. i had agent swarms. a lot of them. let's not get into it</p>
      <p>every build agreed on every login. except musl without pam. that one said yes to things it shouldn't</p>
    </article>

  </div>

<main>

<section id="comic">
  <p>tl;dr</p>
  <figure><img width="1236" height="914" src="https://sshhole.com/comic.svg" alt="six panel comic: 1980 daemon gets a star; 2012 musl crypt returns star instead of NULL; 2016 openssh borrows bob's salt; 2021 alpine changes root from ! to star, narrator: it did not; 2016 to 2026 a cat sleeps on a server; 2026 me and some agents, 300 A's give uid 0"></figure>
</section>

<section id="tryit">
  <h2>try it</h2>
  <p>one debian box, one alpine box. type stuff. it all runs right here in your browser, nothing gets sent anywhere</p>
  <div>
      <div data-libc="glibc">
        <p>debian · glibc</p>
        <p>crypt(pw, bob's salt)</p>
        
        <p>alice's field</p>
        <p>*</p>
        
      </div>
      <div data-libc="musl">
        <p>alpine · musl</p>
        <p>crypt(pw, bob's salt)</p>
        
        <p>alice's field</p>
        <p>*</p>
        
      </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="exposure">
  <h2>are you affected?</h2>
  <ul id="checks">
    <li><span></span><span>is your sshd running on <strong>musl</strong>? (alpine, openwrt, buildroot, yocto)</span></li>
    <li><span></span><span>is it <strong>not using pam</strong>? alpine's <code>openssh-server</code> doesn't by default, or maybe you set <code>UsePAM no</code></span></li>
    <li><span></span><span>are <strong>password logins</strong> still on? they are by default</span></li>
    <li><span></span><span>is there an account with just <code>*</code> as its password? key-only users, ldap users, root on alpine</span></li>
    <li><span></span><span>and some <em>other</em> account with a real password?</span></li>
    <li><span></span><span>then that <code>*</code> account takes any password between 257 and 1024 characters</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>on alpine's official aws and azure images that's the default <code>alpine</code> user, and it has passwordless <code>doas</code>. so it's root</p>
  <p>on debian, ubuntu, fedora or anything else with glibc? going through pam? dropbear? openbsd? you're fine</p>
</section>

<section id="fix">
  <h2>what now</h2>
  <figure>
    <p><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>you</em></p>
    <pre><span># update. openssh 10.6p2 fixes it.</span>
<span>$</span> apk upgrade openssh

<span># can't update yet? turn password logins off.</span>
<span>$</span> echo "PasswordAuthentication no" &gt; /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-no-passwords.conf
<span>$</span> rc-service sshd restart</pre>
  </figure>
</section>

</main>

<div id="check">
    <p>one more thing</p>
    <h2>did you get hacked? check.</h2>
    <figure id="darklog">
      <p><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>root@your-box</em></p>
      <pre><code id="dark-code"></code><span></span></pre>
    </figure>
    <p>see a line like that? an account with no password just took a password</p>
  </div>

<section id="bye">
  <p>if that's the case…</p>
  <p>i'm sorry.</p>
  <p>you got hacked.</p>
  
</section>












</div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[OpenSSH 10.6p2 Critical workaround for alpine without PAM (196 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.openssh.org/releasenotes.html#10.6p2</link>
            <guid>50377976</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 11:40:30 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.openssh.org/releasenotes.html#10.6p2">https://www.openssh.org/releasenotes.html#10.6p2</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50377976">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page">
<hr>
<h3><a href="https://www.openssh.org/txt/release-10.6p2" id="10.6p2">OpenSSH 10.6p2</a> (2026-11-05)</h3>
<pre>Portable OpenSSH 10.6p2 was released on 2026-11-05. It will be available
from the mirrors listed at <a href="https://www.openssh.com/">https://www.openssh.com/</a>.
OpenSSH is a 100% complete SSH protocol 2.0 implementation and
includes sftp client and server support.

Recently the OpenSSH team have received a large number of security
bug reports, many of which are findings from AI models or made with
AI assistance. While many AI reports are determined not to have
security impact when considered in the context of a realistic
threat model, we very much welcome these reports, especially when
combined with human triage, analysis, test-cases and particularly
when accompanied by proposed fixes.

