<p>Looking at the snippet above we can see how the hash table is built. We loop 1024 times to exhaust all possible whitening values for this page of memory. Each iteration we encrypt the page (which will increment the whitening value), get the ciphertext for the LZX decoder data we expect to be in memory and the ciphertext for our malicious data, then add them to the hash tables. On lines 15-16 we can see the <code>HvxFlushSingleTb</code> call that thwarted my own attempt at using a hash table, and on lines 30-31 we can see how the first N bits of the expected ciphertext is used as the hash table key.</p>



<h3 id="improved-cache-flushing">Improved Cache Flushing</h3>



<p>In my original PoC I used a unique feature of the Xbox 360 CPU to <a href="https://icode4.coffee/?p=1081#abusing-the-cpu-for-fun-and-cache" target="_blank" rel="noopener">lock away half of the available L2 cache</a> to try and put as much pressure on it as I could. The L2 locking technique was pivotal in the original PoC but it didn’t put as much pressure on CPU cache as I would have liked. Putting pressure on CPU cache is particularly annoying because kernel mode (where the exploit code is running) cannot use certain cache flushing instructions due to them being privileged and only usable in hypervisor mode. </p>



<p>Additionally, the hypervisor only runs with CPU caching enabled when operating on encrypted memory which makes finding a hypervisor level cache flush primitive difficult. I tried to work around this by finding a <a href="https://icode4.coffee/?p=1081#finding-a-cache-flush-primitive" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hypervisor syscall that would read a large amount of encrypted memory</a>, and I did find one syscall that fit the description, but it wasn’t nearly enough for what I needed. That function also had locks in it which prevented me from executing it on multiple threads simultaneously.</p>



<p>However, there was a kernel mode cache instruction I completely overlooked that Máté had found to work extremely well, <code>dcbz</code> or data cache block set to zero. Aside from just zero’ing out a CPU cache line this instruction has an interesting side effect:</p>



<pre><code>If the cache block is already in the data cache, all of its bytes are cleared to zero. <strong>If it is not in the cache, the block is allocated and initialized directly in the cache</strong> to zero without reading the old contents from main memory.</code></pre>



<p>So if this instruction is used on a memory address that’s not currently in CPU cache the CPU will allocate space for it and potentially evict something else to make room for it. By running the <code>dcbz</code> instruction over large regions of memory it can be used as a cache flush primitive and force old data in CPU cache to be evicted. In Máté’s original implementation he allocated an 0x80000 byte region of memory then looped over it and for every 0x80 bytes (the size of a cache line) called <code>dcbz</code>. </p>



<p>This worked extremely well, however, I found that by increasing the allocation size by 3x and calling <code>dcbz</code> at three distinct locations I was able to assert slightly more pressure on CPU cache which would be important for another improvement I’ll get to later on. Here’s the final implementation:</p>



