<h3>Improved Thread Scheduling</h3>



<p>In part 2 I talked about trying to <a href="https://icode4.coffee/?p=1081#introducing-thread-feng-shui" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule the exploit threads on different hardware threads</a> and how I got the best results by putting them on CPU hardware threads 0 and 1 (which are both on core 0). I don’t really know anything about how CPUs work at a hardware level but I suspected that the load/store memory transactions from both hardware threads on the same core were being queued onto the L2 crossbar in some sort of per-core fashion. Thus by putting the exploit threads on the hardware threads for a single core the writes from the exploit thread and reads from the XKE payload thread would get queued together and reduce latency for loads/stores to RAM, or something like that.</p>



<p>It’s probably not very surprising to anyone reading this but CPUs often have “hidden” instructions, ones not defined by the instruction set architecture (ISA) but implemented by the CPU manufacturer. Sometimes these have a special instruction encoding and other times you’d use an existing instruction in a nonsensical way to tell the CPU to do a special operation.</p>



<p>In the case of PowerPC <a href="https://www.setphaserstostun.org/power8/POWER8_UM_v1.3_16MAR2016_pub.pdf#page=137" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the ISA actually has some well defined instructions</a> for changing the priority of a hardware thread by executing a special OR instruction with all 3 register operands set to the same specific register based on what priority you want the hardware thread to run at. For example:</p>



<div id="urvanov-syntax-highlighter-6aec309065255528220495" data-settings=" minimize scroll-mouseover">
				<table>
					<tbody><tr>
				<td data-settings="show">
					
				</td>
						<td><div><p><span>or</span><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>r</span>1<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>1<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>1<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>#<span> </span><span>Low</span><span> </span><span>priority</span></p><p><span>or</span><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>r</span>2<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>2<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>2<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>#<span> </span><span>Medium</span><span> </span><span>priority</span></p><p><span>or</span><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>r</span>5<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>5<span>,</span><span> </span><span>r</span>5<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>#<span> </span><span>High</span><span> </span><span>priority</span></p></div></td>
					</tr>
				</tbody></table>
			</div>



<p>The Xbox 360 SDK docs describe a few intrinsic macros that can be used to set the priority of the hardware thread the code is running on: <code>__SetHWThreadPriorityLow</code>, <code>__SetHWThreadPriorityMed</code>, and <code>__SetHWThreadPriorityHigh</code>. The actual CPU instructions used by these macros are slightly different than what I listed above (these macros are xenon CPU specific) but that doesn’t really matter. </p>



<p>The SDK docs describe every core as having 32 dispatch slots which are split evenly between hardware threads running at medium priority (which is the default priority). By using the <code>__SetHWThreadPriorityLow</code> and <code>__SetHWThreadPriorityHigh</code> intrinsic macros you can have one hardware thread use 31 of the 32 dispatch slots and the other only use 1. How the dispatch slots relate to number of instructions fed to the CPU execution units I’m not sure (again I have no idea how a CPU pipeline actually works), but being able to go from 16/16 to 1/31 sounds pretty handy for scheduling the exploit threads.</p>



<p>Using these intrinsic macros Máté was able to schedule the exploit threads such that the hardware thread running the XKE payload was only getting 1 of the 32 dispatch slots while the ciphertext overwrite thread got the remaining 31 slots. The result was that the CPU was now spending the overwhelming majority of time in the ciphertext detection loop than it was actually running the XKE payload.</p>



<h3>Improved Ciphertext Overwrite Detection</h3>



<p>The last improvement was something I came up with while working on the Peer Pressure exploit to try and make the Bad Update exploit as stable as I could get it. In the original PoC I had discovered the compressed bootloader data the XKE payload operates on was compressed into 15 blocks with all but the last block having a decompressed size of 0x8000 bytes. If the XKE payload attack was successful part of the last segment of the hypervisor would be overwritten with the decompressed data for one or more of the compressed blocks. By <a href="https://icode4.coffee/?p=1081#update-data-side-channel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">observing the ciphertext for the last segment of the hypervisor in two distinct locations</a> I could determine if the last block of compressed data was written to it by the XKE payload.</p>


<div>
<figure data-wp-context="{&quot;imageId&quot;:&quot;6aec30906a2de&quot;}" data-wp-interactive="core/image" data-wp-key="6aec30906a2de"><img decoding="async" width="1201" height="274" data-wp-class--hide="state.isContentHidden" data-wp-class--show="state.isContentVisible" data-wp-init="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--click="actions.showLightbox" data-wp-on--load="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--pointerdown="actions.preloadImage" data-wp-on--pointerenter="actions.preloadImageWithDelay" data-wp-on--pointerleave="actions.cancelPreload" data-wp-on-window--resize="callbacks.setButtonStyles" src="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/hypervisor_code_block_write-1.png" alt="" srcset="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/hypervisor_code_block_write-1.png 1201w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/hypervisor_code_block_write-1-300x68.png 300w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/hypervisor_code_block_write-1-768x175.png 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1201px) 100vw, 1201px"><figcaption>Using ciphertext to determine when the last compressed block was written to memory.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<p>The attack was setup such that if the last block of compressed data was written to the hypervisor at a specific offset it would change the instructions for one of hypervisor syscalls to a write-what-where primitive I could use to get full hypervisor code execution. However, there was a flaw in how the ciphertext was being observed which not only led to false positives that would crash the console but also missed iterations where the last block was written to memory successfully.</p>



