<p>Above is the original implementation for the exploit thread that ran the XKE payload and checked for ciphertext changes in the last segment of the hypervisor. The lines relevant to ciphertext observation have been highlighted. Because the same thread is both running the XKE payload and checking for ciphertext changes it can only detect if the ciphertext has changed at all and not how many times it changed. Put more simply: I could only infer if one of the compressed blocks was written to memory, but not how many blocks were written. With that in mind lets run through two scenarios that could happen when running an iteration of the XKE payload while attempting to get compressed block 14 written to memory.</p>



<h4>Missing Successful Writes</h4>



<p>While running the XKE payload the race attack is successful on some block N such that N &lt; 14 (0x8000 bytes get written) and successful on block 14 (less than 0x8000 bytes). Upon returning from <code>HvxKeysExecute</code> we observe the ciphertext for two locations in the target write zone and see that the ciphertext has changed in both locations. We can’t infer that block 14 was written after block N because we don’t have the ability to observe how many times the ciphertext changed. Thus we consider this a failed iteration even though it was successful.</p>


<div>
<figure data-wp-context="{&quot;imageId&quot;:&quot;6aec30906a53d&quot;}" data-wp-interactive="core/image" data-wp-key="6aec30906a53d"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1201" height="274" data-wp-class--hide="state.isContentHidden" data-wp-class--show="state.isContentVisible" data-wp-init="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--click="actions.showLightbox" data-wp-on--load="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--pointerdown="actions.preloadImage" data-wp-on--pointerenter="actions.preloadImageWithDelay" data-wp-on--pointerleave="actions.cancelPreload" data-wp-on-window--resize="callbacks.setButtonStyles" src="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/missed_write-1.png" alt="" srcset="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/missed_write-1.png 1201w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/missed_write-1-300x68.png 300w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/missed_write-1-768x175.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1201px) 100vw, 1201px"><figcaption>Ciphertext observations when block 14 and any other block are written.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<h4>False Positive Write</h4>



<p>Assume we have just executed an iteration of the XKE payload that exhibits the missed successful write behavior for some block N and block 14. The last segment of the hypervisor now contains the data for block 14 and the tail data for some block N. We run another iteration of the XKE payload and this time only block N is written. When we observe the ciphertext changes for this iteration we’ll see the ciphertext at the first location has change but the ciphertext at the second location hasn’t because it already contained the ciphertext for the tail data of block N from the previous iteration.</p>


<div>
<figure data-wp-context="{&quot;imageId&quot;:&quot;6aec30906a73e&quot;}" data-wp-interactive="core/image" data-wp-key="6aec30906a73e"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="851" height="274" data-wp-class--hide="state.isContentHidden" data-wp-class--show="state.isContentVisible" data-wp-init="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--click="actions.showLightbox" data-wp-on--load="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--pointerdown="actions.preloadImage" data-wp-on--pointerenter="actions.preloadImageWithDelay" data-wp-on--pointerleave="actions.cancelPreload" data-wp-on-window--resize="callbacks.setButtonStyles" src="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/invalid_write_detected.png" alt="" srcset="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/invalid_write_detected.png 851w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/invalid_write_detected-300x97.png 300w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/invalid_write_detected-768x247.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 851px) 100vw, 851px"><figcaption>Ciphertext observations when block N is written after a missed write.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<p>This gets detected as a write for block 14 and the exploit will try to execute some hypervisor code assuming the instruction pattern for the target syscall has been changed to the write-what-where pattern found in bock 14. However, the instruction pattern is actually random instructions found in block N and will cause the console to crash.</p>



<h4>The New Detection Method</h4>



<p>Now that the cache flushing primitive was significantly improved it was possible to observe the ciphertext changes for each block of compressed data that was successfully written to the target region of hypervisor memory. The solution I came up with for the detecting if block 14 was written to memory was quite simple: I created a new thread that continuously watched the ciphertext for changes and counted how many changes were observed.</p>



<p>If the counter reached 15 once the XKE payload was done executing then we know all 15 blocks were written to memory and thus the last block had to have been written. If we observed anything less we simply consider it a failed iteration and try again. Here’s the code for the new ciphertext detection thread:</p>



<div id="urvanov-syntax-highlighter-6aec309065258558422905" data-settings=" minimize scroll-mouseover">
