<h2>Too Much Pressure</h2>



<p>The new ciphertext observation function worked great but there was a new problem it introduced. Due to the amount of pressure I was putting on CPU cache the console would crash about 30% of the time when executing the XKE payload. When the XKE payload runs it takes the compressed update data buffer and does an in-place relocation to move it from unprotected memory to protected memory so it can’t be tampered with asynchronously. After the XKE payload runs its main logic it’ll do another in-place relocation to move the update data back to unprotected memory before returning.</p>



<p>After modifying the XKE payload and inserting a bunch of <code>printf</code> calls I was able to determine that the console would crash in a function I called <code>HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical</code> when trying to move the update data from protected memory back to unprotected memory. The function works by looping over a region of memory and for each CPU cache line worth of data (128 bytes) it will: </p>



<ol>
<li>Read the entire cache line into CPU registers using the protected source address.</li>



<li>Invalidate both data and instruction caches for the source address, zero the cache line for the destination address.</li>



<li>Write the data back to memory using the unprotected destination address.</li>
</ol>



<p>Here’s the disassembly for the inner loop of the <code>HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical</code> function:</p>


<div>
<figure data-wp-context="{&quot;imageId&quot;:&quot;6aec30906aa40&quot;}" data-wp-interactive="core/image" data-wp-key="6aec30906aa40"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="671" height="680" data-wp-class--hide="state.isContentHidden" data-wp-class--show="state.isContentVisible" data-wp-init="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--click="actions.showLightbox" data-wp-on--load="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--pointerdown="actions.preloadImage" data-wp-on--pointerenter="actions.preloadImageWithDelay" data-wp-on--pointerleave="actions.cancelPreload" data-wp-on-window--resize="callbacks.setButtonStyles" src="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/image-26.png" alt="" srcset="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/image-26.png 671w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/image-26-296x300.png 296w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 671px) 100vw, 671px"><figcaption>Disassembly for the HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical function.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<p>In the modified version of the XKE payload I added a bunch of <code>printf</code> calls in a re-implemented version of this function so I could monitor were it would crash:</p>


<div>
<figure data-wp-context="{&quot;imageId&quot;:&quot;6aec30906ac00&quot;}" data-wp-interactive="core/image" data-wp-key="6aec30906ac00"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="673" height="930" data-wp-class--hide="state.isContentHidden" data-wp-class--show="state.isContentVisible" data-wp-init="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--click="actions.showLightbox" data-wp-on--load="callbacks.setButtonStyles" data-wp-on--pointerdown="actions.preloadImage" data-wp-on--pointerenter="actions.preloadImageWithDelay" data-wp-on--pointerleave="actions.cancelPreload" data-wp-on-window--resize="callbacks.setButtonStyles" src="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/memory_relocate_printf-1.png" alt="" srcset="https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/memory_relocate_printf-1.png 673w, https://icode4.coffee/wp-content/uploads/memory_relocate_printf-1-217x300.png 217w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 673px) 100vw, 673px"><figcaption>Disassembly for HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical with printf patches applied.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<p>After running a few dozen iterations I was able to determine that the CPU would crash when trying to write data back to the unprotected destination address on the line highlighted in red. As I mentioned earlier in the <a href="https://icode4.coffee/?p=1746#improved-cache-flushing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">improved cache flushing</a> section I made some modifications to Máté’s original implementation to put more pressure on cache which made the new ciphertext observation method more reliable.</p>



<p>I don’t know exactly what’s happening here that causes the CPU to crash when trying to write data back to the unprotected destination address. Perhaps one of the cache invalidation instructions didn’t fully complete before the store instruction was executed, or maybe there’s some edge case with respect to the custom memory pathways when the CPU is starved for cache. Regardless, I did manage to find a way to work around this issue.</p>



<h3>The New Detection Method v2</h3>



<p>Since the crash happens after we would have been able to observe all 15 blocks of data being written to hypervisor memory the solution I came up with was to immediately stop the cache flushing thread and drop the L2 locks once the ciphertext change counter hits 15. This reduces the pressure on CPU cache before the call to <code>HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical</code> which prevents it from crashing. Here’s the updated implementation of the ciphertext observation thread logic:</p>



<div id="urvanov-syntax-highlighter-6aec309065259204572531" data-settings=" minimize scroll-mouseover">
				<table>
					<tbody><tr>
				<td data-settings="show">
					<div><p>1</p><p>2</p><p>3</p><p>4</p><p>5</p><p>6</p><p>7</p><p>8</p><p>9</p><p>10</p><p>11</p><p>12</p><p>13</p><p>14</p><p>15</p><p>16</p><p>17</p><p>18</p><p>19</p><p>20</p><p>21</p><p>22</p><p>23</p><p>24</p><p>25</p><p>26</p><p>27</p><p>28</p><p>29</p><p>30</p><p>31</p><p>32</p><p>33</p><p>34</p><p>35</p><p>36</p><p>37</p><p>38</p><p>39</p><p>40</p><p>41</p><p>42</p><p>43</p><p>44</p><p>45</p><p>46</p><p>47</p><p>48</p><p>49</p><p>50</p><p>51</p><p>52</p><p>53</p><p>54</p><p>55</p><p>56</p></div>
				</td>
						<td><div><p><span>void</span><span> </span><span>CipherTextObservationThreadProc</span><span>(</span><span>THREAD_ARGS</span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>pArgs</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Lock half of the available L2 cache and pathways to put pressure on the CPU. This causes data in L2 cache to</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// age out more quickly and improves cipher text detection rate.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>BYTE</span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>pL2LockBuffer</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>LockAndThrashL2</span><span>(</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Setup our hypervisor cipher text watch pointer.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>volatile</span><span> </span><span>DWORD</span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>pCipherTextPtr</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>volatile</span><span> </span><span>DWORD</span><span>*</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>0xA0030000</span><span> </span><span>+</span><span> </span><span>HV_SEG3_OVERWRITE_OFFSET</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Flush cache and fetch the starting value.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>__dcbf</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>void</span><span>*</span><span>)</span><span>pCipherTextPtr</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>DWORD </span><span>lastCipherTextValue</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span>pCipherTextPtr</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>volatile</span><span> </span><span>DWORD</span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>pBlockCounter</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>volatile</span><span> </span><span>DWORD</span><span>*</span><span>)</span><span>&amp;</span><span>pArgs</span><span>-&gt;</span><span>CipherTextBlockCounter</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Loop until we're told to exit.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>while</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>pArgs</span><span>-&gt;</span><span>bRunCacheThrashing</span><span> </span><span>!=</span><span> </span><span>FALSE</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>int</span><span> </span><span>i</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>i</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>10000</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>i</span><span>++</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Flush any cached data for the cipher text observation pointer.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>__dcbf</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>void</span><span>*</span><span>)</span><span>pCipherTextPtr</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Fetch the cipher text for the area of the hypervisor we want to overwrite and check if another block has been written.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>DWORD </span><span>test</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span>pCipherTextPtr</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>test</span><span> </span><span>!=</span><span> </span><span>lastCipherTextValue</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>lastCipherTextValue</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>test</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>(</span><span>*</span><span>pBlockCounter</span><span>)</span><span>++</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>}</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Once we observe 15 different cipher text values stop thrashing cache immediately.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>*</span><span>pBlockCounter</span><span> </span><span>==</span><span> </span><span>15</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Unlock the L2 cache and free the scratch buffer.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>UnlockL2</span><span>(</span><span>pL2LockBuffer</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>pL2LockBuffer</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>NULL</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Flag to stop thrashing cache.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>pArgs</span><span>-&gt;</span><span>bRunCacheThrashing</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>FALSE</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>goto</span><span> </span><span>Exit</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>}</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>}</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>}</span></p><p><span>Exit</span><span>:</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>pL2LockBuffer</span><span> </span><span>!=</span><span> </span><span>NULL</span><span>)</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>{</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// Unlock the L2 cache and free the scratch buffer.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>UnlockL2</span><span>(</span><span>pL2LockBuffer</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>}</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>// We must call ExTerminateThread since we don't use the normal thread startup routine.</span></p><p><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span>ExTerminateThread</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span>;</span></p><p><span>}</span></p></div></td>
					</tr>
				</tbody></table>
			</div>



<p>I moved the L2 cache locks into the ciphertext observation thread which we can see on line 5. Once we observe all 15 blocks of data have been written to memory we drop the L2 locks on line 36, and signal to the cache flushing thread to immediately terminate on line 40.</p>



<p>There’s still an edge case where we don’t observe all 15 blocks being written and thus won’t be able to reduce pressure on CPU cache before the call to <code>HvpRelocateProtectedToPhysical</code>. But this didn’t seem to matter. With all the improvements the exploit is usually successful in the first run of the XKE payload and at worst it may take 2-5 iterations, but I didn’t encounter any crashes when testing. It’s still possible it could happen but I think it’s very rare it does.</p>



<h2>Conclusion</h2>



<p>So there you have it, thanks to all the improvements from ihatecompvir, InvoxiPlayGames, and Máté the Bad Update exploit is now instantaneous and has nearly 100% success rate, and you only need to copy a few files to a USB stick to hack your console. When I released the original PoC I made some statements like “I don’t think this can be optimized any further” mostly to avoid endless comments from non-technical people spit balling random useless ideas. The truth is I always thought it could be optimized further but I didn’t expect it would ever reach the point it’s at now.</p>



<p>I think the biggest thing I learned here is that I should have taken a break after I completed the first iteration of Bad Update and came back with a clear mind to try and refine it. Having just found one of the only two bugs in the Xbox 360 hypervisor I put a lot of pressure on myself to get this exploit working at all. It took a lot of effort and sleepless days to pull it off and in the end I got tunnel vision chasing issues that didn’t really matter which distracted me from the real issues at hand. Had I taken a break after the initial PoC and came back a few weeks later I probably could have had something more refined for the initial release even if it wasn’t as refined as it is now.</p>



<p>Any ways, that does it for part 3. You can find the source code for the Bad Update improvements on my <a href="https://github.com/grimdoomer/Xbox360BadUpdate/tree/v1.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>. Part 4 which covers the Peer Pressure exploit will be out soon so stay tuned for that!</p>




									
																		
								</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
